{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-120409",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The time of melting $t$ is inversely proportional to the temperature $T,$ so we have\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{k}{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that it takes $10\\,min$ for an ice cream to melt at ${30}^{∘}C.$ We can use this information to solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{k}{30} \\\\ 10\\cdot 30 & =k \\\\ 300 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{300}{T}.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the time it takes for the ice cream to melt at ${20}^{∘}C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{300}{20}=15\n$$\n\nTherefore, the time it takes for the ice cream to melt when $T={20}^{∘}C$ is $t=15\\,min.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
